.TH TRUNCATE 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
truncate \- shrink or extend files to a given size
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B truncate
.RB [ \-c ]
.BI "\-s " size
.IR file ...
.br
.B truncate \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B truncate
sets the length of each
.I file
to
.I size
bytes.
It cuts off the data beyond that length and extends a shorter file with
zero bytes.
The data below the new length is kept.
It is the
.BR busybox (1)
applet, which has fewer options than GNU
.BR truncate ;
see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
The files are processed in order.
A
.I file
that does not exist is created, empty, and then extended, unless
.B \-c
is given.
A
.I file
that cannot be opened or resized is reported on standard error, and the
remaining files are still processed.
The exit status is then 1.
The messages have the forms
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
truncate: \fIfile\fR: open: \fIreason\fR
truncate: \fIfile\fR: truncate: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
Nothing is written to standard output.
The size is read before any file is touched, so an invalid
.I size
creates nothing.
With no
.BR \-s ,
no
.IR file ,
or an invalid option,
.B truncate
prints a usage message on standard error and exits with status 1.
.SS Sizes
A
.I size
is a number in decimal digits, with an optional suffix that multiplies
it.
A sign, white space, a fraction, and hexadecimal notation are not
accepted, and leading zeros do not make a number octal:
.B 010
is ten.
Suffixes are case-sensitive:
.TP
.BR k ", " K
1024.
.TP
.BR kB ", " KB ", " kD ", " KD
1000.
.TP
.B M
1048576.
.TP
.BR MB ", " MD
1000000.
.TP
.B G
1073741824.
.TP
.BR GB ", " GD
1000000000.
.PP
A number that is malformed, or that does not fit in 64 bits unsigned,
ends
.B truncate
with status 1 and one of these messages, the second for a number that
its suffix makes too large:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
truncate: invalid number \(aq\fIsize\fR\(aq
truncate: number \fIsize\fR is not in 0..18446744073709551615 range
.EE
.RE
.PP
A
.I size
above 9223372036854775807 is accepted, but resizing then fails with
.BR "Invalid argument" ,
after the
.I file
is created if it did not exist.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR "\-cs 1M" ,
and may follow the
.I file
operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
The argument of
.B \-s
may be attached, as in
.BR \-s1M ,
or be the next argument.
Repeating
.B \-s
keeps the last
.IR size .
.B \-\-
ends the options.
There are no long options apart from
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B \-c
Do not create files.
A
.I file
that does not exist is skipped without a message, and does not affect
the exit status.
This holds for any missing path, including one whose directory does not
exist.
Other failures to open a
.IR file ,
such as a directory or a read-only file, are still errors.
.TP
.BI "\-s " size
Set the length of each
.I file
to
.I size
bytes.
This option is required.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument only, print a usage message on standard output and
exit with status 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS
When it is
.BR 1 ,
Windows may show a critical-error dialog for a
.I file
on a drive with no disk in it.
Otherwise busybox suppresses the dialog, and the file fails to open.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded when busybox starts, as needed
when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A pattern that matches nothing is left as it is.
A busybox process started with the variable unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
arguments as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
A file mode creation mask in octal.
When it includes the owner write bit,
.BR 200 ,
a file that
.B truncate
creates is read-only.
The run that creates it still extends it, but later runs fail to open it
with
.BR "Permission denied" .
In the kit's shell,
.B truncate
uses the shell's mask instead; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first
.I file
operand ends the options, so that a later
.B \-s
is a file name.
.PP
Messages written to a console also depend on
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_ALL ,
and
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
as for every busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every
.I file
was resized, or skipped by
.BR \-c .
This includes
.B \-\-help
as the first argument.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option or
.IR size ,
no
.BR \-s ,
no
.IR file ,
or a
.I file
that could not be opened or resized.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a file of 1000000 zero bytes, or resize an existing one to that
length:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ truncate \-s 1MB blob.bin
.EE
.RE
.PP
Empty a file without deleting it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ truncate \-s 0 build.log
.EE
.RE
.PP
Keep the first 512 bytes of a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ truncate \-s 512 image.bin
.EE
.RE
.PP
Empty the files that exist, and ignore those that do not:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ truncate \-c \-s 0 old.log cache.tmp
.EE
.RE
.PP
Remove the last 16 bytes of a file.
There are no relative sizes, so the shell computes the new length:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ truncate \-s $(($(stat \-c %s data.bin) \- 16)) data.bin
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR truncate :
.IP \(bu 2
The size is absolute.
A leading
.BR + ,
.BR \- ,
.BR < ,
.BR > ,
.BR / ,
or
.B %
is not accepted.
.IP \(bu 2
The only options are
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-s ,
and
.BR \-\-help .
.B \-o
(sizes in blocks),
.B \-r
(copy the size of another file), and the other long options, such as
.B \-\-size
and
.BR \-\-version ,
are invalid.
.IP \(bu 2
The only suffixes are those listed under
.BR Sizes .
Lowercase
.B m
and
.BR g ,
the larger units such as
.BR T ,
and the
.B iB
forms such as
.B KiB
are invalid.
.PP
Extending a file allocates the new range on disk and fills it with
zeros, so it takes time and disk space in proportion to the growth.
A very large size keeps running until the disk is full or
.B truncate
is interrupted, and an interrupted run leaves the file partly extended.
On NTFS,
.B truncate
first marks the file sparse, and the mark stays on the file, but the new
range is allocated like any other data, not left as a hole.
.PP
A directory operand fails with
.BR "Is a directory" ,
and a read-only file with
.BR "Permission denied" ,
with or without
.BR \-c .
A file named
.B \-
is an ordinary file name, not standard output.
.IR /dev/null ,
.IR /dev/zero ,
and
.I /dev/urandom
are accepted, and resizing them succeeds without effect.
.PP
The kit's
.B sh
runs
.B truncate
inside a shell process, without starting busybox anew, so
.B truncate
uses the shell's file mode creation mask, which the shell's
.B umask
command sets, and ignores a
.B BB_UMASK
assignment on its command line.
.PP
File names may use drive letters and backslashes.
They may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR dd (1),
.BR du (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR stat (1)
